    "reply": "## KRAS Molecular Function\n\nThe KRAS gene encodes a protein with GTPase enzymatic activity [1] [4]. Specifically, it belongs to the small GTPase superfamily, which hydrolyzes guanosine triphosphate (GTP) to regulate cellular signaling [11]. The encoded protein functions as a molecular switch in signal transduction pathways.\n\n## Oncogenic Activation and Disease\n\nA single amino acid substitution creates an activating mutation that produces a transforming protein [11]. This constitutively active KRAS protein persistently stimulates downstream pathways controlling cell proliferation and survival, leading to tumorigenesis [26]. The transforming protein is implicated in various malignancies including lung adenocarcinoma, pancreatic ductal carcinoma, and colorectal carcinoma [11] [26].\n\n## Therapeutic Targeting\n\nKRAS was long considered \"undruggable\" due to its smooth protein surface lacking traditional drug-binding pockets [27]. However, recent developments include allele-specific inhibitors targeting KRAS(G12C) mutations, such as sotorasib and adagrasib, which have shown clinical efficacy [27]. Additionally, heterobifunctional small-molecule degraders have been designed that can target multiple oncogenic KRAS alleles through proteolytic degradation rather than enzymatic inhibition [30].",
    "dur": 27.62
   },
